---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudySection from "../../components/CaseStudySection.astro";

// Rebuilt from Figma "coach casestudy" frame (node 752:6382)
// https://www.figma.com/design/2nYhgPKrVIuNd9J4G02Mae/ai-app?node-id=752-6382
// GIF sections use local files in public/images/work/coach/ (pulled from the
// frame's animated fills, not Figma's static exports).

const meta = [
	{ label: "Timeline", value: "Aug 2024 - Present" },
	{ label: "Role", value: "Lead Product Designer" },
	{ label: "Tools", value: "Figma, Magic Patterns, Lovable, Claude API, Vercel" },
];

const gif = "shrink-0 w-[160px] sm:w-[200px]";
const panel =
	"flex w-full flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-6 rounded-[4px] bg-[#1a1b1d] px-4 py-8";
const caption = "max-w-full font-space-grotesk text-xs text-[#e8e7e3]";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="Coach"
	heroSrc="/images/work/coach/hero.png"
	heroAlt="Coach mobile app calculator screen"
	meta={meta}
>
	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Outcome" heading="From guesswork to a system beginners trust">
		<div class={panel}>
			<img src="/images/work/coach/firefox_97GKwV7RDQ.gif" alt="Coach empty dashboard on a phone" class={gif} />
			<img src="/images/work/coach/firefox_cYtMVMMMOm.gif" alt="Coach warm-up calculator in Manual mode" class={gif} />
			<img src="/images/work/coach/firefox_tyS3EknInC.gif" alt="Coach warm-up calculator in AI mode" class={gif} />
		</div>
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			Coach shipped with four core features: workout logging, a warm-up calculator, a progression
			calculator, and account creation. It's live and functional at coachtest.vercel.app. The
			bigger win is what it replaced: an AI-generated strategy card now explains the reasoning
			behind every recommended weight, so beginners build training intuition instead of dependence
			on the app.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Context" heading="Taking the guesswork out of getting stronger">
		<img
			src="/images/work/coach/context.png"
			alt="Early Coach desktop dashboard build in Lovable"
			class="aspect-[960/479] w-full rounded-[4px] object-cover"
		/>
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			New lifters know they need to train progressively, but most apps either overwhelm them with
			logging or give nothing beyond a timer. Coach started as a fast build in Lovable to test the
			concept, and it worked well enough to prove the idea, but the early screens showed what
			moving fast costs: dashboards packed with more data than a lifter needed mid-set, UI
			elements crowded together with no breathing room, and a mobile view that barely held up on
			an actual phone. That first pass validated the concept but made it clear the interface
			needed a real design process behind it, not just more features.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Problem" heading="Existing apps track your lifts. None of them teach you how to lift.">
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">No direction for new lifters.</strong> Knowing what to log doesn't
				help if you don't know how to train.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">Warm-ups were an afterthought.</strong> The few tools that existed
				felt outdated and disconnected from the rest of your workout.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">Progress locked behind paywalls.</strong> Structured training was
				out of reach for people just starting out.
			</p>
		</div>
		<img
			src="/images/work/coach/problem.png"
			alt="Coach mobile screen showing the workout logging and calculator interface"
			class="aspect-[960/479] w-full rounded-[4px] object-cover"
		/>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Solution" heading="A calculator that thinks like a coach">
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			Coach replaces guesswork with a structured workflow: AI-calculated warm-up sets prepare your
			nervous system before heavy lifts, double progression logic tells you exactly when to add
			weight, and the whole system is designed around how beginners actually build strength, not
			how advanced lifters track it.
		</p>
		<div class={panel}>
			<img src="/images/work/coach/firefox_9nxtYEoi5B.gif" alt="Coach progression calculator in AI mode" class={gif} />
			{/* TODO: two supporting stat-card crops (Coach's Strategy card + Ramp-Up row) — not available as clean Figma exports */}
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Design Decision" heading="The gym isn't a desk job">
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">Tried:</strong> the first build was desktop-only, a clean dashboard-style
				layout for logging sets and viewing progress.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">Result:</strong> clean enough, but wrong context. Every user would
				be standing at a squat rack holding their phone, not sitting at a desk.
			</p>
			<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
				<strong class="font-bold">Fix:</strong> rebuilt mobile-first, so the core logging and calculator
				flows work one-handed, standing up, mid-session.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="flex w-full items-center justify-center rounded-[4px] bg-[#1a1b1d] px-4 py-8">
			{/* TODO: design splits this into separate desktop (630w) + mobile (322w) crops with captions "The original desktop build for coach" / "Early Mobile design" */}
			<img
				src="/images/work/coach/desktop-to-mobile.png"
				alt="Side-by-side comparison of the original desktop build and the early mobile redesign for Coach"
				class="aspect-[960/508] w-full rounded-[4px] object-contain"
			/>
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Design Decision" heading="Warm-up sets calculated before you touch the bar">
		<div class={panel}>
			<div class="flex w-[468px] max-w-full flex-col items-center gap-2">
				<img src="/images/work/coach/firefox_cYtMVMMMOm.gif" alt="Coach warm-up calculator in Manual mode" class={gif} />
				<p class={`${caption} text-center`}>
					<strong class="font-bold">Generates</strong> exact warm-up sets at 50%, 75%, and 85% of working
					weight automatically, each stage building toward the working set.
				</p>
				<p class={`${caption} text-center`}>
					<strong class="font-bold">Manual/AI</strong> toggle keeps both modes on one screen, so lifters
					can switch without losing their inputs mid-session.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="flex w-[468px] max-w-full flex-col items-center gap-2">
				<img src="/images/work/coach/firefox_tyS3EknInC.gif" alt="Coach warm-up calculator in AI mode" class={gif} />
				<p class={`${caption} text-center`}>
					<strong class="font-bold">AI</strong> mode reads what actually happened in your last set, weight
					and reps completed, and recommends increase, hold, or decrease.
				</p>
				<p class={`${caption} text-center`}>
					<strong class="font-bold">Coach's</strong> Strategy card surfaces that reasoning inline, so beginners
					build intuition over time instead of dependence on the app.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Design Decision" heading="Knowing your warm-up weight is only half the equation">
		<div class={panel}>
			<div class="flex w-[468px] max-w-full flex-col items-center gap-2">
				<img src="/images/work/coach/firefox_Kk67Uo5ZW4.gif" alt="Coach progression calculator in Manual mode" class={gif} />
				<p class={`${caption} text-center`}>
					<strong class="font-bold">Most</strong> beginners track their lifts but have no framework for
					knowing when to push heavier or when to pull back.
				</p>
				<p class={`${caption} text-center`}>
					<strong class="font-bold">Progression</strong> uses the same pill-tab pattern as the warm-up
					calculator, so switching between tools doesn't mean relearning the app.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="flex w-[468px] max-w-full flex-col items-center gap-2">
				<img src="/images/work/coach/firefox_9nxtYEoi5B.gif" alt="Coach progression calculator in AI mode" class={gif} />
				<p class={`${caption} text-center`}>
					<strong class="font-bold">A</strong> new lifter who hits 11 reps on an 8-rep target has no instinct
					for what that means next week, logging data alone doesn't give direction.
				</p>
				<p class={`${caption} text-center`}>
					<strong class="font-bold">AI</strong> mode translates weight, target reps, and reps completed
					into a clear next working weight, with the reasoning shown inline.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="What's next" heading="Building the social layer">
		<div class={panel}>
			<img src="/images/work/coach/firefox_97GKwV7RDQ.gif" alt="Coach account-sync prompt on a phone" class={gif} />
			{/* TODO: supporting "Create Account" modal crop (442w) — not available as a clean Figma export */}
		</div>
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			Losing months of logged progress because you switched phones or cleared your browser would
			kill the habit entirely. Account creation syncs workout history to a database so users never
			start from zero. A friends layer builds on top of that, adding social accountability as a
			second reason to stay consistent.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="What's next" heading="Medals borrowed from competitive games">
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			Weight progression is slow, a beginner might train for weeks before their numbers move
			meaningfully, which is exactly when most people quit. Medals add a second progression track
			that rewards consistency over performance, so users feel advancement even when the plates
			aren't moving. Borrowed from ranked systems in competitive games, where visible milestones
			keep players returning regardless of skill level.
		</p>
		<div class={panel}>
			<img src="/images/work/coach/firefox_fbijwWf5SN.gif" alt="Coach achievements and medals screen" class="shrink-0 w-[200px] sm:w-[240px]" />
			{/* TODO: supporting zoomed medal-card crop (454w) — not available as a clean Figma export */}
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Outcome" heading="What shipped">
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			Coach shipped with four core features: workout logging, warm-up calculator, progression
			calculator, and account creation. The database is currently inactive, but the app is live
			and functional at coachtest.vercel.app.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Outcome" heading="Try it live">
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			Coach is live and functional. Try the warm-up and progression calculators yourself, right
			here.
		</p>
		<div class="flex justify-center overflow-x-auto py-2">
			<iframe
				src="https://coachtest.vercel.app"
				style="width: 390px; height: 844px; border-radius: 24px; border: none;"
				title="Coach live app"></iframe>
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection eyebrow="Outcome" heading="Looking back">
		<p class="font-space-grotesk text-base text-[#1a1a1a]">
			I'd define the core feature set before writing a line of code. Design decisions that came
			from mid-build user feedback led to better features, but also extended the timeline. The
			lesson: validate the feature list early, then build with constraints.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>
</CaseStudyLayout>
